'''Masahito''' ({{nihongo|Masahito-tennō|正仁天皇}}; born 23 October 1967) is the 126th and current [[Emperor of Okaiken]]. He acceded to the Chrysanthemum Throne on 7 January 1985, following the death of his father, Emperor Shōwa II. His reign is designated as the '''Jutsu''' era ({{nihongo|Jutsu|術}}, meaning "Technique" or "Art"), reflecting the national focus on industrial mastery and technical precision.

Masahito's reign is historically distinct for being divided into two radically different constitutional phases. From 1985 to 2011, he reigned as a symbolic figurehead under the post-war Constitution of 1970. Following the [[Referendums of 2011]], which led to the "Jutsu Restoration," his status was legally elevated to [[Head of State]], granting him significant reserve powers and an active role in the governance of the nation. He is the first Sanese monarch since 1946 to exercise direct political influence, a shift that has defined modern Sanese politics.

==Early life and succession==
Born in 1967 at the Togu Palace in [[Okami]], Masahito was the first son of then-Crown Prince Akihito (later [[Emperor Go-Shōkō]]). His childhood coincided with Okaiken's rapid post-war reindustrialization. Unlike his ancestors who were educated by private tutors, Masahito attended the Gakushūin School system, where he was noted for a reserved and observant demeanor.

In 1985, at the age of 17, his life changed abruptly when his father died of heart failure. Masahito ascended the throne while still a minor under Sanese law, requiring a brief regency council until his 18th birthday later that year. His coronation in November 1985 was a subdued affair, reflecting the economic austerity of the time and the constitutional constraints that limited the monarchy's budget and visibility.

==The Silent Years (1985–2011)==
For the first twenty-six years of his reign, Emperor Masahito strictly adhered to the restrictions of the 1970 Constitution, which defined the Emperor solely as the "symbol of the State." He performed ceremonial duties—signing documents, receiving foreign ambassadors, and awarding honors—but was forbidden from making political statements. This period, often called the "Silent Years," coincided with Okaiken's political stagnation, characterized by a series of weak, short-lived prime ministers and bureaucratic gridlock.

Despite these limitations, Masahito quietly cultivated relationships with the captains of Okaiken's industry. He frequently visited factories and research laboratories rather than art galleries or flower shows, subtly signaling his priority: the nation's physical strength. He married Michiko Shoda, the daughter of a prominent steel magnate, in 1998, a union that was seen as a solidification of the bond between the Throne and the industrial sector.

==The 2011 Restoration==
[[File:Emperor Masahito TV Broadcast.png|thumb|left|300px|Emperor Masahito broadcasting a message to the nation after he was restored as emperor.]]
The turning point of his reign came with the [[Referendums of 2011]]. Amidst a severe economic depression and a parliamentary deadlock that left the nation without a budget for four months, public dissatisfaction with the "weak state" system reached a boiling point. A coalition of conservative and reformist parties proposed a constitutional amendment to restore the Emperor's authority as a check against parliamentary incompetence.

The referendum passed with 62% of the vote. On May 1, 2011, the amended constitution came into effect. In a historic televised address—his first time speaking directly to the nation on political matters—Masahito declared the beginning of the "Jutsu Era," calling for a return to "discipline, endurance, and competence." He famously donned the traditional ''sokutai'' court dress for the official portrait of the restoration, reviving the visual language of the pre-war monarchy to signal the return of imperial dignity.

==Reign as Head of State (2011–Present)==
===Domestic role===
[[File:Masahito 2011 Official.png|thumb|right|250px|Emperor Masahito's portrait after the restoration]]
Since the Restoration, Emperor Masahito has exercised his "Reserve Powers" with calculated restraint but decisive effect. He holds weekly audiences with the Prime Minister, where he reportedly questions government policy with the rigor of a corporate auditor. In 2015, he famously refused to sign a budget bill that reduced infrastructure spending, forcing the Diet to redraft the legislation—an act that solidified the reality of his new powers.

He has focused his reign on the "Forge State" ideology, heavily promoting the heavy industry, shipbuilding, and defense sectors. He is the patron of the annual "Emperor's Prize for Industrial Excellence," personally touring the winning factories. His stoic, mustache-wearing appearance has become a symbol of the "New Okaiken"—serious, masculine, and unyielding.

===Foreign relations===
Masahito has pursued a "Realist Diplomacy." Unlike the "smiling diplomacy" of the symbolic era, Masahito's state visits are often accompanied by trade delegations and defense pact signings. He has worked to strengthen ties with nations that can provide raw materials for Okaiken's industries while maintaining a "polite distance" from the cultural influence of neighboring powers like Peilan.

==Personal life==
The Emperor is married to [[Empress Michiko]], and they have three children: [[Crown Prince Tomohito]] (born 2002), [[Princess Kiko]] (born 2006), and [[Prince Teruhito]] (born 2010).

Masahito is known for his personal austerity. He reportedly refuses to have the heating turned on in the Imperial Palace until December to "share the endurance" of the citizenry. His primary hobby is traditional Sanese archery (''Kyūdō''), which he practices daily at dawn. He is also an amateur scholar of military history, with a specific interest in the logistics of the [[Sengoku period]].

